+Keep one authoritative board per event. Editors submit commands over HTTPS; the server assigns each committed edit one event revision. Browsers display that revisioned view and receive committed changes over self-hosted WebSocket connections, giving low-latency updates without a dependency on a hosted realtime service. Subsecond updates would be useful, but are not required.
 
-Delivery, change storage, and browser recovery must be designed together after this choice. Polling would favor cursor queries; self-hosted sockets would need connection management and replay; hosted delivery would need publication reconciliation and provider credentials. Neither operating cost nor minimum operational burden has been declared the overriding criterion.
+Delivery, change storage, and browser recovery are designed together around this route. The change store retains replayable event revisions and a reliable publication path from commit to broadcast. Delivery owns the WebSocket sessions — connection capacity, per-connection authorization, and broadcast of new revisions. Browser sync owns subscription, reconnect, and catch-up: after a dropped connection a browser re-subscribes and replays missed revisions to reach the latest board. Owning the socket tier trades added connection and reconnect operations for independence from a realtime vendor.
 
