 ## Key Design Decisions
 
 - **Commands over HTTPS, one authoritative board per event.** Editors submit commands to the server, which assigns each committed edit one event revision. Peer-to-peer or browser-side merging was rejected: the tenets require one authoritative revision regardless of delivery route, and offline editing is a non-goal.
+- **Committed changes are delivered over self-hosted WebSockets.** Delivery pushes each new event revision to subscribed browsers over WebSocket connections the project operates itself. This gives low-latency updates and keeps the board free of a hosted realtime vendor, accepting the cost of owning connection capacity and reconnect/catch-up handling. Five-second HTTPS polling (simpler and stateless, but repeated reads and several seconds of lag) and a hosted realtime service (least connection ops, but recurring fees and provider coupling) were rejected.
